Clearing the file flags is a no-op, and a no-op cannot fail.

sys_chflags and sys_fchflags returned ENOTSUP for every call, with a comment saying that is what a
filesystem without file flags reports and that applications must accept it. They must, for a request
that asks for something. THIS ONE ASKS FOR NOTHING: getattrlist here always reports flags == 0,
because Linux has no BSD file flags, so chflags(path, 0) is asking for the state the file is already
in. Everything that clears flags before deleting does exactly that, which is what rm -rf and
installers do.

Measured, from the suite that has been in this tree unable to run:

    FAIL [14]: lchflags(path, 0) returns 0 (errno=45: Operation not supported)
    FAIL [16]: chflags(path, 0) returns 0 (errno=45: Operation not supported)

and the same suite is green with this. A NONZERO value is still ENOTSUP: those flags cannot be
stored, and a success there would be a lie the caller can read back. The setattrlist path in
helper/xattr/setattrlist_generic.c accepts any value deliberately, for Nix; that inconsistency is
known and this is the more honest half of it.
diff -ur old/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/unistd/chflags.c new/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/unistd/chflags.c
--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/unistd/chflags.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/unistd/chflags.c
@@ -8,10 +8,22 @@
 
 long sys_chflags(const char* file, int flags)
 {
+	// CLEARING THE FLAGS IS A NO-OP, AND A NO-OP CANNOT FAIL. getattrlist here always reports
+	// flags == 0, because Linux has no BSD file flags, so a caller asking for 0 is asking for the
+	// state the file is already in. Answering ENOTSUP to that broke everything that clears flags
+	// before deleting, which is what rm -rf and installers do, and it is what
+	// tests/syscall/test_setattrlist_flags.c failed on: FAIL lchflags(path, 0) returns 0
+	// (errno=45: Operation not supported).
+	//
+	// A NONZERO value is still ENOTSUP: we cannot store those flags, and a success there would be
+	// a lie the caller can read back. (The setattrlist path in
+	// helper/xattr/setattrlist_generic.c accepts any value deliberately, for Nix; that
+	// inconsistency is known, and this is the more honest half of it.)
+	//
 	// TODO: We could implement this via ioctl(fd, FS_IOC_GETFLAGS/FS_IOC_SETFLAGS),
 	// but it requires root and this functionality is not all that important.
-	// 
-	// Returning ENOTSUP indicates that this filesystem doesn't support file flags,
-	// which is true on NFS for example, so applications must accept this error return.
+	if (flags == 0)
+		return 0;
+
 	return -ENOTSUP;
 }
